    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0198" n="100" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg100"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Gemmell, Nina E./27: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-04-07T10:00:00+08:00">April 7, 1930—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:18 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>23. My 22 April 5, 1 p.m.<anchor n="84" xml:id="d62-f1"/> Telegram received this
                    morning from Changsha by China Inland Mission states that Miss Gemmell has been
                        released.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d62-f2"/></p>
                <p>Legation, Nanking and Shanghai informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d62-f1" n="84" xml:id="d62-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d62-f2" n="85" xml:id="d62-f2-content">
                <p>She was held from March 23 to April 4. In despatch No. 948, April 12, 1930, the
                    Consul General at Hankow reported to the Legation “that $1,100 in cash was paid
                    to the bandits and that it is believed this payment brought about the release of
                    Miss Gemmell.” (393.1111 Gemmell, Nina E./36)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
